du'u that is created by applying djuno2 to djuno3, and there's a djuno-du'u
definition, where the speaker knows djuno2, which pertains to djuno3 in
some way. The former is in some sense "incomplete"; {lo se djuno} is not a
complete bridi syntactically but is not a "fact" semantically either. The
latter is redundant, and could just as easily be done with srana. (That is,
{mi djuno lo du'u broda noi srana ko'a} =3D=3D {mi djuno lo du'u broda kei
ko'a} and {mi djuno lo srana be ko'a} =3D=3D {mi djuno fi ko'a}).
